    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Verify for yourself that $Anxiety = Stress + 0.1 \\times Time$ holds perfectly in every row. Notice the additional `StressSurvey` column—a survey-based proxy for measuring stress levels without expensive blood tests. The proxy has a *monotonic* (and sorta-kinda *linear*) relationship with actual stress (see @fig-stress-proxy).\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## 📝 Methodological Note: The Contrived Nature of This Example\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a contrived example designed to illustrate the dangers of linear regression:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Blood test stress levels** have a perfectly linear relationship with anxiety (by design)\n",
        "- **Survey stress responses** have a non-linear relationship with anxiety (also by design)\n",
        "\n",
        "In the real world, there is no reason to believe linearity holds for either measurement method. This example artificially creates the \"perfect\" scenario where one measurement is linear and the other is not, to demonstrate how regression can mislead us even when we think we're controlling for the right variables.\n",
        "::::"
      ],
      "id": "253de84a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-stress-proxy\n",
        "#| fig-cap: StressSurvey as a proxy for actual Stress levels\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import seaborn as sns\n",
        "\n",
        "sns.set_style(\"whitegrid\")\n",
        "plt.rcParams['figure.figsize'] = (7, 4)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "ax.plot(observDF['Stress'], observDF['StressSurvey'], \n",
        "        linewidth=1, color='purple', marker='o', markersize=12)\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"StressSurvey seems a decent (monotonic) proxy for actual Stress\")\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"Actual Stress Level\")\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel(\"Stress Survey Response\")\n",
        "ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-stress-proxy",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Analysis Tasks\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## 🎯 The Benchmark: True Coefficients\n",
        "\n",
        "For every regression you run below, compare your estimated coefficients to these true values:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $\\beta_0 = 0$ (intercept) | $\\beta_1 = 1$ (Stress) | $\\beta_2 = 0.1$ (Time)\n",
        "\n",
        "Are your estimates close? If not, your model is telling a wrong story -- even if R-squared is high and p-values are small.\n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Questions for 75% Grade\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Bivariate Regression: Anxiety on StressSurvey.** Run a bivariate regression of Anxiety on StressSurvey. What are the estimated coefficients? How do they compare to the true relationship?\n",
        "\n",
        "Below shows a bivariate regression of Anxiety on Stress Survey. The estimated coefficients are as follows. The intercept is off by -1.524 (the correct intercept was 0), and the stressSurvey coefficient of 1.047 was surprisingly close to the correct coefficient of 1! It was only off by 4.7%. "
      ],
      "id": "d4887e86"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: bivariate-regression-stress-survey\n",
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
        "from sklearn.metrics import r2_score\n",
        "\n",
        "x1 = observDF['StressSurvey'].values\n",
        "y = observDF['Anxiety'].values\n",
        "\n",
        "X = x1.reshape(-1, 1)\n",
        "model_biv = LinearRegression()\n",
        "model_biv.fit(X, y)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Coefficient (StressSurvey): {model_biv.coef_[0]:.3f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Intercept: {model_biv.intercept_:.3f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"R-squared: {r2_score(y, model_biv.predict(X)):.3f}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))\n",
        "ax.scatter(x1, y, alpha=0.7, edgecolors='black', linewidths=0.5)\n",
        "x_line = np.linspace(x1.min(), x1.max(), 100)\n",
        "y_line = model_biv.predict(x_line.reshape(-1, 1))\n",
        "ax.plot(x_line, y_line, color='C3', linewidth=2, label='Line of best fit')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('StressSurvey')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Anxiety')\n",
        "ax.set_title('Perceived Level of Stress is Highly Correlated with Anxiety')\n",
        "ax.legend()\n",
        "ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "bivariate-regression-stress-survey",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
